    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>From 1044 to 1287 Bagan was the capital and the political, economic and cultural centre of the Pagan Empire, and its rulers and wealthy subjects built over 10,000 religious monuments in an area of 104 km2.</li><li>Building peaked between about 1150 and 1200, when most of the largest monuments went up; the earliest vaulted temples date to the 11th century, and the vaulting seems to have developed at Bagan itself.</li><li>Bagan outlived its empire as a settlement and a pilgrimage place, but earthquakes damaged it badly in 1975 and 2016, and its restorations of the 1990s were widely condemned before it was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 2019.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The vaulting techniques of the Bagan era were lost in later periods: in the 18th century King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a> tried to build the Mingun Pagoda as a spacious vaulted temple but failed, as the craftsmen and masons of his time had lost the knowledge of vaulting and keystone arching.</p></aside>
    </section>

